--  Acting on whether something worked.
--
--  A command is a procedure: `run ("false");` produces no value. Without a way
--  to ask afterwards, a program could run something and never learn whether it
--  worked -- which is most of what a shell script does. `Status` is that ask,
--  and it is `$?` under an Ada name.
--
--  The number is the one exit-status model `Adash.Execution` documents and the
--  shell itself exits with: 0 for success, what an external program chose,
--  126 for something found and not executable, 127 for something not found,
--  128 + n for a program a signal killed. A script forwarding a status does not
--  have to translate it.

run ("true");
put_line (Status);

run ("false");
put_line (Status);

--  Which makes a subprogram that answers the question directly, in the
--  language rather than by a convention of the shell.
function Ran (What : String) return Boolean is
begin
   run (What);
   return Status = 0;
end Ran;

if Ran ("true") then
   put_line ("that one worked");
end if;

if not Ran ("false") then
   put_line ("that one did not");
end if;

--  It is an ordinary Integer: it compares, it goes into a variable, and it does
--  arithmetic.
Last : Integer := Status;

put_line (f"the last one exited {Last'Image}");
